# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b3), edited. beatrice_wells's first conversation.

~ start
set beatrice_wells_met
Narrator: Pine shavings and warm linseed oil scent the open workshop. Beatrice planes a curved beam to a three-part tune hummed from the rafters.
beatrice_wells [smile]: Mind the sawdust, friends, and come right in! Every joint has a song in it if you cut the mortise true.
-> menu

~ menu
beatrice_wells [smile]: Now then, what can the Wells bench build or mend for you tonight?
* Browse their wares -> shop_open
* Ask about her carpentry and the family -> work
* Ask about the new chapel she is building -> village
* Ask what catches her eye in the village buildings -> rumour
* [Perception DC 13] Glance at the charcoal sketches pinned under her bench -> secret_ok | secret_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ shop_open
beatrice_wells [smile]: Spokes, handles, chests, or stout ash poles, all seasoned dry and sung over!
shop
-> menu

~ work
beatrice_wells [smile]: Branden turns the lathe, the four little ones sweep and fetch pegs, and I shape the frames. A workshop that sings together never splits the grain.
-> menu

~ village
beatrice_wells [neutral]: Brother Weston asked for high, clear eaves on the new Brightsinger chapel. Old Ways folk mutter, but good oak answers to any honest chisel.
-> menu

~ rumour
beatrice_wells [sly]: Look at that old house on the hill in the moonlight. Not a rafter sits square, yet the whole pitch leans together without a creak.
-> menu

~ secret_ok
Narrator: Beneath the chapel plans lie dozens of frantic charcoal sketches of the same impossible, crooked dovetail joint.
beatrice_wells [weary]: That angle ought to collapse, yet it holds like iron. If I could puzzle out how the builder bent the king-post, my chapel spire would touch the stars.
-> menu

~ secret_fail
beatrice_wells [smile]: Only timber tallies and choir sheets under there, friend, nothing so grand as the finished roof.
-> menu
